From: Husarion <support@husarion.com>
Subject: [PATCH] SHM: the statistics strip finds the statistics submessage by the submessage chain

RTPSMessageGroup::send() appends the 60-byte statistics submessage (id 0x80)
only to RTPS messages shorter than 65511 bytes (65535 - RTPSMESSAGE_DATA_MIN_LENGTH:
an unfragmented DATA above 64 KiB must stay the last submessage).
SharedMemTransport::send() then calls remove_statistics_submessage(), and
get_statistics_message_pos() decides that the message carries the submessage
from one byte, send_buffer[size - 60] == 0x80. A message of 65511 bytes or more
never carries one, and its byte at size - 60 is payload: when it happens to be
0x80, the transport cuts the last 60 bytes of real data. Such messages exist only
with a maxMessageSize above 65535 on the SHM transport (UDP datagrams stay below
the threshold). An unfragmented sample between 64 KiB and maxMessageSize is
delivered 60 bytes short about once in 256 random samples (a constant 0x80 fill:
every sample), and nothing heals it. A fragmented sample exposes every DATA_FRAG
message: best effort loses about 6 % of 1 MiB random samples, reliable heals
by retransmission except when the byte stays 0x80 in every resend (a 0x80 fill
never arrives). set_statistics_submessage_from_transport() (UDP and TCP, through
OutputTrafficManager) uses the same test and writes a locator, a timestamp and
counters at that position, so a TCP transport with a maxMessageSize above 65535
would overwrite 56 payload bytes in place (reasoned from the code, not reproduced).
Present in 2.14.6 and 2.14.7 and on the 2.14.x branch (2026-10-15), from the
backport of the sender-side size guard (eProsima/Fast-DDS#5742).

get_statistics_message_pos() now walks the submessage chain from the RTPS header
the way MessageReceiver reads it (length in the endianness of the flags' E bit,
padded to 4; a zero length on anything but INFO_TS and PAD is the last
submessage, which extends to the end of the message), and returns the position
only when a submessage starts exactly at size - 60 with the 0x80 id and the
statistics submessage's 56-byte length. That is the definition of "the last
submessage is the statistics submessage", so it holds for every message shape,
including encrypted messages (the submessage is appended after encoding) and big
DATA submessages (length 0, nothing follows them). The one-byte test stays as the
fast path: a message whose byte is not 0x80 is never walked. Alternatives:
- a length test cannot decide it: with the submessage a message is 80 to 65570
  bytes long, without it 65511 or more, so 65511 to 65570 is ambiguous both ways;
- carrying a flag from RTPSMessageGroup to the transport changes the public
  TransportInterface::send() signature and the library's ABI;
- 3.x strips by buffer identity (the submessage is its own NetworkBuffer and
  remove_statistics_buffer() looks at buffers.back()), which needs the gather-send
  transport API that 2.14 does not have.
Private header only, no ABI or wire change: a patched process strips exactly what
a stock one strips from every message that carries the submessage.

---
--- a/src/cpp/statistics/rtps/messages/RTPSStatisticsMessages.hpp
+++ b/src/cpp/statistics/rtps/messages/RTPSStatisticsMessages.hpp
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include <fastdds/rtps/common/Types.h>
 #include <fastdds/rtps/messages/CDRMessage.h>
 #include <fastdds/rtps/messages/RTPSMessageCreator.h>
+#include <fastdds/rtps/messages/RTPS_messages.h>
 
 #define FASTDDS_STATISTICS_NETWORK_SUBMESSAGE 0x80
 
@@ -160,8 +161,38 @@
         uint32_t pos = send_buffer_size - statistics_submessage_length;
         if (FASTDDS_STATISTICS_NETWORK_SUBMESSAGE == send_buffer[pos])
         {
-            // only succeed if the message is properly formatted
-            statistics_pos = pos;
+            // The byte alone proves nothing: RTPSMessageGroup appends the statistics submessage
+            // only to messages shorter than 65511 bytes, and in a longer one (a DATA above 64 KiB,
+            // a DATA_FRAG of a large maxMessageSize) this is a payload byte. Walk the submessage
+            // chain from the RTPS header as MessageReceiver reads it, and only succeed when a
+            // submessage starts exactly here with the statistics submessage's length.
+            using namespace eprosima::fastrtps::rtps;
+            uint32_t walk = RTPSMESSAGE_HEADER_SIZE;
+            while (walk < pos)
+            {
+                const octet id = send_buffer[walk];
+                const bool little_endian = (send_buffer[walk + 1] & 0x01) != 0;
+                const uint32_t length = little_endian ?
+                        (send_buffer[walk + 2] | (send_buffer[walk + 3] << 8)) :
+                        ((send_buffer[walk + 2] << 8) | send_buffer[walk + 3]);
+                if ((0 == length) && (INFO_TS != id) && (PAD != id))
+                {
+                    // This submessage extends to the end of the message: nothing follows it
+                    break;
+                }
+                walk += RTPSMESSAGE_SUBMESSAGEHEADER_SIZE + ((length + 3u) & ~3u);
+            }
+            if (walk == pos)
+            {
+                const bool little_endian = (send_buffer[pos + 1] & 0x01) != 0;
+                const uint32_t length = little_endian ?
+                        (send_buffer[pos + 2] | (send_buffer[pos + 3] << 8)) :
+                        ((send_buffer[pos + 2] << 8) | send_buffer[pos + 3]);
+                if (statistics_submessage_data_length == length)
+                {
+                    statistics_pos = pos;
+                }
+            }
         }
     }
 
